tring then JSON-encode it. In my mind, I'll write the code and then manually escape for JSON. But easier: In the response, I'll construct the JSON manually. I'll write the code with proper indentation, then escape newlines and quotes.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    segments = payload['segments']\n    budget = payload['budget']\n    \n    # validate fields\n    for i, seg in enumerate(segments):\n        if not all(k in seg for k in ('name','importance','minimum','capacity')):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Segment {i} missing required field\")\n        if not isinstance(seg['name'], str) or not seg['name']:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Segment {i} name invalid\")\n        for field in ('importance','minimum','capacity'):\n            val = seg[field]\n            if not isinstance(val, int) or val <= 0:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Segment {i} {field} must be positive integer\")\n        if seg['minimum'] > seg['capacity']:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Segment {i} minimum exceeds capacity\")\n    \n    names = [seg['name'] for seg in segments]\n    importances = {seg['name']: seg['importance'] for seg in segments}\n    capacities = {seg['name']: seg['capacity'] for seg in segments}\n    minima = {seg['name']: seg['minimum'] for seg in segments}\n    \n    sum_min = sum(minima.values())\n    sum_cap = sum(capacities.values())\n    \n    if budget < sum_min:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Budget {budget} below total minimum {sum_min}\")\n    if budget > sum_cap:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Budget {budget} exceeds total capacity {sum_cap}\")\n    \n    allocations = {name: minima[name] for name in names}\n    remaining = budget - sum_min\n    \n    # Precompute order index for tie-breaking\n    order = {name: idx for idx, name in enumerate(names)}\n    \n    while remaining > 0:\n        best_score = -1.0\n        best_name = None\n        best_idx = None\n        for name in names:\n            if allocations[name] < capacities[name]:\n                # score = importance / (next_position)\n                # next_position = allocations[name] + 1\n                score = importances[name] / (allocations[name] + 1)\n                idx = order[name]\n                if score > best_score or (score == best_score and idx < best_idx):\n                    best_score = score\n                    best_name = name\n                    best_idx = idx\n        if best_name is None:\n            # Should not happen because budget <= total capacity and we have remaining\n            # but just in case break\n            break\n        allocations[best_name] += 1\n        remaining -= 1\n    \n    # Build result\n    result = {\n        \"allocations\": {name: allocations[name] for name in names},\n        \"total\": budget,\n        \"segments\": names\n    }\n    return result\n```\n\nCheck: The while loop uses float division. That's fine. However, note that we use order dictionary for tie-breaking; we set best_idx initially to large number, but we compare idx < best_idx. Since best_idx is initially None, we need to handle that. In the code above, I set best_name, best_score, best_idx. I set best_score = -1.0, best_name = None, best_idx = None. Then in condition: if score > best_score or (score == best_score and idx < best_idx): but if best_idx is None, comparing idx < None will raise TypeError. So we need to handle initial state. One way: set best_idx = float('inf')? Or use a tuple for comparison. Simpler: first iteration we just assign. Use a flag. Or pre-initialize best_idx to None and check if best_idx is None then assign without comparison. Let's adjust:\n\n```\nif best_name is None or score > best_score or (score == best_score and idx < best_idx):\n    best_score = score\n    best_name = name\n    best_idx = idx\n```\n\nThat works because if best_name is None, the condition short-circuits and we assign. So we need to set best_name = None initially. Then ins